#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093159} :activity/id #uuid "a4a9429f-a3d9-515f-9589-b6a42b363bed" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050797 :topic/id #uuid "67a4c8b0-845c-55dc-ad90-cd6044fd5591" :topic/math-academy-id 969} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093153 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092289 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092136 :question/id #uuid "6fc6a60d-11e9-56c1-9328-0179059d4c56" :question/math-academy-id "e-8389"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d3383cb-2322-5ed0-bd5b-631596d72af9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092928}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"} :step/id #uuid "44a89e4d-3f22-5cc4-9a84-768d256c3961" :step/math-academy-id 31243 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093154}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093154 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092290 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092137 :question/id #uuid "a8951542-ff6f-5b96-a287-b4c7f928b572" :question/math-academy-id "e-8390"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69ebb184-52aa-5fde-8956-50b69fde3561" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092927}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"} :step/id #uuid "9c37bc5f-181a-519b-99c1-ff45fd6ec950" :step/math-academy-id 31244 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093155}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093155 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092291 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092138 :question/id #uuid "13299051-2164-5d54-b90e-52c7a8f1d7f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8391"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d68a11bd-7a2d-5f1f-8be7-c8904fdfb3e7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092929}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "8ad7b4f1-5165-5f3a-b42e-2ad2eda221fc" :step/math-academy-id 31245 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093160}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092140 :question/id #uuid "4fbdc166-4225-5fa5-aaa2-0f09073e8e2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8768"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092931}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"} :step/id #uuid "0ad68353-836a-50b6-ac4a-7e9b88a98c32" :step/math-academy-id 32687} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093454 :tutorial/content "The hyperbolic functions have inverse functions that are easy to define. The **inverse hyperbolic functions** are the following:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n- The **inverse hyperbolic sine**, defined as\n$\\sinh^{-1}{x} = \\arsinh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$.\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic sine function is given below.![](images/1f/1f07f38b11cf297e099341df2ab062d4691c4502098c060de565b70f1ea7e360.png)\n- The **inverse hyperbolic cosine**, defined as\n$\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty)$.\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic cosine function is given below.![](images/60/60607a764c50807dcb0f4f05328d900c626079654995cffbaa84fa2214788835.png)\n- The **inverse hyperbolic tangent**, defined as\n$\\tanh^{-1}{x} = \\artanh{x} = \\dfrac12\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right), \\qquad x \\in (-1, 1)$.\nThe graph of the inverse hyperbolic tangent function is given below.![](images/96/96b4f7aa06ee165751dcaa2101e9dea104d95d5bebdaea7bc4f317d9160b5a7a.png)\n\nFor example, to calculate $\\arcosh(\\sqrt2)$ we substitute $x$ for $\\sqrt2$ in the formula for $\\arcosh$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\narcosh (\\sqrt{2}) &= \\ln (\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{(\\sqrt{2})^{2} - 1}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{2 - 1}) \\\\\n&= \\ln (\\sqrt{2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "afeb7395-4188-5e5e-8d91-200a65a1cbb9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5206 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6f5b5a8c-0e08-5ad0-9503-4a131c8d5c2e" :step/math-academy-id 32690 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093153}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093160 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093455 :tutorial/content "We can derive the formulas from inverse hyperbolic sine, cosine, and tangent from the definition of these base functions.\n\nFor example, let's show that\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\cosh^{-1}{x} = \\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\big(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\big), \\qquad x \\in [1, \\infty)\n$$\n\nRecall that\n\n$$\n\\cosh{x} = \\dfrac12 \\left(e^x + e^{-x} \\right)\n$$\n\nFirst, let\n\n$$\ny = \\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\ny\\geq 1\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\cosh y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, now we have that\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny \\geq 0\n$$\n\nNext, we use the definition of $\\cosh x$ and bring everything to the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{y} + e^{-y}) \\\\\n2x &= e^{y} + e^{-y} \\\\\n2xe^{y} &= e^{2y} + 1 \\\\\ne^{2y} - 2xe^{y} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(e^{y})^{2} - 2x(e^{y}) + 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLetting $Y = e^{y}$, we form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nY^{2} - 2xY + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(Y - x)^{2} - x^{2} + 1 &= 0 \\\\\n(Y - x)^{2} &= x^{2} - 1 \\\\\nY - x &= ± \\sqrt{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\nY &= x ± \\sqrt{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\ne^{y} &= x ± \\sqrt{x^{2} - 1} \\\\\ny &= \\ln (x ± \\sqrt{x^{2} - 1})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want\n\n$$\ny\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe select the positive solution and obtain our formula for $\\arcosh$ shown above." :tutorial/id #uuid "edf0d23e-aec4-5b77-befa-2433291ed5af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5219 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formulas for the Inverse Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "c7e64c95-cb61-5b1e-ac3c-639ae6f35144" :step/math-academy-id 32709 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186093158}}] :activity/title "The Inverse Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186092452}]}
